<!--
  Deliberately empty, and it is worth saying what is NOT here.

  THERE IS NO `BOOT_COMPLETED` RECEIVER, because there are already two and a
  third would be a third answer to one question. `androidx.work`'s own
  `RescheduleReceiver` re-enqueues this module's periodic work after a reboot,
  and `expo-task-manager`'s `TaskBroadcastReceiver` reconstructs `TaskService`,
  which restores the persisted location task. Both are in the merged manifest
  already.

  THERE IS NO `RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED` EITHER, and that one surprised us.
  `androidx.work:work-runtime` declares it in its own manifest, and
  `expo-background-task` has depended on WorkManager since it was added — so
  the permission has been merged into this app since long before this module
  existed, and expo-task-manager's boot receiver has been firing all along.
  Declaring it here would be a second declaration of something already true
  and would read, to the next person, as the thing that made boot work.

  What that receiver does on boot is restore the location task WITHOUT its
  foreground service — `LocationTaskConsumer.maybeStartForegroundService`
  refuses while no Activity is foregrounded — so a rebooted handset resumes
  throttled background delivery rather than the dense trail. That is the whole
  reason `TrackerWatchdogWorker` also nudges the salesman to open the app: only
  the foreground start puts the real tracker back.

  The one permission this module would otherwise want,
  `android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS`, is requested by `expo-notifications`
  and declared through `app.json`, which keeps every permission this app asks a
  salesman for in ONE file somebody can read — the same decision recorded in
  `phone-setup`'s own manifest.
-->
